  "Question": "A basketball player made \\(5\\) baskets during a game. Each basket was worth either \\(2\\) or \\(3\\) points. How many different numbers could represent the total points scored by the player?",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(2\\)",
    "B": "\\(3\\)",
    "C": "\\(4\\)",
    "D": "\\(5\\)",
    "E": "\\(6\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "E",
  "Solution": "If all five baskets were worth \\(2\\) points, the total would be \\(5\\cdot2=10\\) points. Each basket worth \\(3\\) instead adds \\(1\\) point.\n\nThere can be \\(0,1,2,3,4,\\) or \\(5\\) such baskets, giving the possible totals\n\\[\n10,11,12,13,14,15.\n\\]\nEach total is attainable, and different numbers of 3-point baskets give different totals. There are \\(\\boxed{6}\\) possible totals, choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{E}}\\).",
